// Showcase — 30s, the reference render for the cinematic camera system.
//
//   ./verse --cinematic --output showcase.mp4 --shot-script assets/shots/showcase.json \
//           --res 1920x1080 --fps 24 --samples 24 --letterbox --grain 0.02 \
//           --relativistic 0
//
// Authoring notes, each one learned the hard way:
//
//  * EVERY key is placed relative to the SUN, along the galactic pole. An
//    earlier draft placed the far keys relative to the galactic CENTRE, which
//    teleported the camera from 22 AU off the Sun to 63 light years from
//    Sgr A* — inside the core, which blew the frame out to white. One origin
//    per continuous move.
//  * Keys are spaced GEOMETRICALLY once the camera leaves the system.
//    Catmull-Rom takes a key's tangent from its neighbours, so adjacent
//    segments of wildly different length make the spline overshoot.
//  * Rising along the pole from the Sun arrives face-on to the disc with no
//    separate move; the Sun's 26 kly offset stops mattering billions of AU out.
//  * SHUTTER closes for the fast legs. At these speeds a star crosses the
//    frame within one frame interval, and a 180-degree shutter renders that as
//    N discrete ghosts ("double vision") rather than a streak — no practical
//    sample count fixes it. Closing the shutter shortens the streak instead.
//  * PACING: the galactic interior is a featureless haze, so the climb through
//    it is compressed and the spiral reveal lands at ~21s and is then held for
//    the last third. The eye wants to rest on the payoff, not on the transit.
//  * timescale drops to 1 at galactic scale: nothing visibly animates out
//    there, and sim time costs real render time (motion blur re-runs the
//    integrator once per accumulation sample).
